Under normal conditions, the stability of MOFs arises from a variety of factors such as thermodynamic factors, kinetic factors and other factors in the operating environment.3,19 Thermodynamic factors are mainly related to the metal–ligand coordination bond strength.3,19 The coordination bond strength might be predicted by Pearson's hard so acid base (HSAB) theory. In the de novo synthesis of MOFs, ligands with relatively high $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ (e.g. azoles) easily produce robust frameworks with low-valent metal ions, while linkers with relatively low $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ (e.g. carboxylic acids) tend to bind with high-valent metal ions to give stable structures.3,19,20 The inertness of metal clusters generally endows MOFs with excellent chemical stability, as conrmed in many MOFs, such as MIL-101(Cr).21 However, even with the same metal cluster and framework topology, the chemical stability of some MOFs decreases with the lengthening of the linker and the enlargement of pore size (as observed in UiO-66 series, SUMOF-7 series, etc.).22,23 This is mostly caused by kinetic factors, which are mainly related to the rigidity of the linker, coordination number, surface hydrophobicity, framework interpenetration, etc.3,19 In general, dense and rigid frameworks constructed by rigid and highly connected building blocks (metal ions/clusters and ligands) usually demonstrate high stability due to their high tolerance toward partial lattice collapse.19,24,25 Surface hydrophobicity prevents the adsorption of water into pores and/or the condensation of water around the metal clusters, which enhances the MOF stability in the presence of water.3 On the other hand, improved stability from framework interpenetration is likely to be caused by increased steric hindrances to ligand displacement.3 Further, the inuence of reduced systematic energy of the interpenetrated structure cannot be ignored.26 Beyond these, the operating environment as an external factor also plays an important role in MOF stability. For instance, moisture-labile MOF-5 can retain its crystallinity upon heating at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .27 Hence, the assessment of MOF stability for identication of suitable MOFs should be varied to meet the specic conditions in an operating environment.
The development of stable MOFs can be divided into several important stages and that began with the advent of the rst porous MOF (MOF-5).27 In the early stage, the study of MOF stability was mainly focused on their thermal and mechanical stability.21,28–35 With the in-depth understanding of the framework collapse of MOFs in air,36 the development of stable MOFs entered the second stage. Numerous water-stable MOFs were reported based on their structural designability and chemical tunability. In the third stage, different techniques of crystal growth and crystal structure determination gave rise to a large number of extraordinarily stable MOFs, which effectively widened their application ranges and beneted their commercialization.
Over the past two decades, various promising methods have been developed to tune MOF stability, especially chemical stability, which might be the most important and signicant prerequisite of MOFs for their diverse applications.19,37–39 Therefore, we believe that it is a good time for us to provide a systematic review of recent advances in the preparation of stable MOFs and their applications (Scheme 1). Our discussions begin with a summary of strategies that have been developed for improving the chemical stability of MOFs while emphasizing the key factors in the synthesis of stable MOFs and MOF-based materials. Next, the unique attributes of stable MOFs and their extensive applications, including adsorption and separation, heterogeneous catalysis, uorescence sensing, biological and medical application, and proton conductivity, are introduced. At the end of the review, a summary and proposed future developments in this research eld will be presented. Hopefully, this review will inspire the enthusiasm of scientists in chemistry and materials science and interdisciplinary researchers toward this eld.
## 2. Chemical stability
Although many MOFs are vulnerable to structural destruction even in an ambient atmospheric environment due to the lability of coordination bonds between metal ions and ligands, a growing number of MOFs with exceptional chemical stability have been reported in recent years. In general, two main types of approaches have been adopted to improve the chemical stability of MOFs. One is the de novo synthesis of stable unknown MOFs and the other is to improve the stability of existing MOFs. The chemical stabilities of some representative MOFs are summarized in Table 1.
## 2.1 Governing MOFs towards high stability via de novo synthesis
It is widely believed that the degradation of MOFs generally has two aspects, namely, the metal–ligand bond breaking, and the formation of products that are more stable as compared with pristine MOFs.40,41 Hence, the chemical stability of MOFs depends strongly on the intrinsic structures of MOFs (internal factors), including the charge density of metal ions, connection numbers of metal ions/clusters, basicity and conguration as well as hydrophobicity of ligands, etc. Following these considerations, judicious selection of metal nodes and design of linkers enable the construction of MOFs with sound stability.42
2.1.1 High-valent metal-containing MOFs. Based on the HSAB principle, with the same coordination environments, high-valent metals with high charge density (hard acids), including $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , ${\bf A l}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , etc., tend to coordinate with O donor ligands (hard bases) (Fig. 1) to form MOFs with strong coordination bonding, thus usually presenting good chemical stability. Despite the relatively low $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of carboxylate linkers, these high-valent metal ions oen require more linkers to balance the charges. This can lead to a high connection number of metal clusters and further enhance the stability of the resultant MOFs. In addition, the strong coordination bonds and the low $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of carboxylate linkers endow the high-valent metal ion-containing MOFs with a decent level of acidic stability.
As an early example, F´erey and co-workers fabricated a $\mathrm{Cr}\mathrm{\left(m\right)}.$ based MOF, MIL-101(Cr), with very large pore sizes (2.9 and 3.4 $\mathrm{nm})$ and Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area $(\sim4000$ $\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ via the combination of targeted chemistry and computational design.43 By virtue of the inertness of $\mathbf{Cr-}$ carboxylate oxygen bonds, MIL-101(Cr) is highly resistant to the attack of acid and alkali, making it stable in aqueous solutions of $\mathrm{pH}=0{-}12$ for 2 months.44 Using a similar strategy, a variety of high-valent metal-containing MOFs with high chemical stability have been reported, including MIL-100(Cr), MIL-53(Cr), UiO series, MIL-125, Al-TCPP, PCN series, etc.22,45–68 However, the strong M–O bonds oen lead to the poor crystallinity and the small sizes of resultant MOF particles. This increases the difficulty of structural characterization with single-crystal X-ray diffraction. For this reason, most of the crystal structures of the above-mentioned MOFs are determined by using powder X-ray diffraction.
